        "body": "When refreshing your wardrobe at retailers like Westside, FabIndia, Ajio, Myntra, or your neighborhood master tailor, use this decision framework to avoid generic retail traps: What to Skip: • Stark, paper-white business shirts made from 100% stiff non-iron polyester blends. They trap tropical heat and create an unflattering pale cast. • Cool cement-gray polo shirts and henleys. They look drab on golden, olive, and dusky complexions and show perspiration instantly. • Muddy beige and light khaki trousers that match your exact leg skin tone. If your pants and skin tone have zero value separation, your silhouette loses all definition. • Cheap synthetic poly-silk festive kurtas that gleam artificially under banquet flash and make skin appear oily. What to Buy: • Ivory, pearl, and unbleached cotton shirts in breathable slub cotton or linen blends. They brighten the face without harsh blue reflection. • Deep jewel-toned casual tees and kurtas—including forest green, rich plum, midnight navy, and brick rust. These provide high-value contrast against warm skin. • Textured handloom layers like Khadi cotton overshirts, linen waistcoats, and lightweight Nehru jackets in earthy terracotta, sage green, and charcoal. • Dark, grounded bottoms such as deep olive chinos, dark indigo selvedge jeans, and espresso brown linen trousers that anchor outfits cleanly."
