        "heading": "The Origins of the Four-Season Framework: Carole Jackson and the 1980s Model",
        "body": "If you have spent any time exploring fashion styling, personal color theory, or digital aesthetics, you have inevitably encountered the four seasons: Winter, Spring, Summer, and Autumn. People proudly declare on social media: \"I am a True Autumn,\" or \"I finally discovered I am a Bright Winter!\" To understand where this system came from—and why it often confuses Indian shoppers—we must look at its historical roots. The concept of Seasonal Color Analysis was originally popularized in the 1980s by American beauty consultant Carole Jackson in her bestselling book Color Me Beautiful. Jackson built upon Johannes Itten’s color wheel theories from the Bauhaus art movement, dividing human complexions into four palettes named after the natural seasons: 1. Winter: Cool undertones, high contrast (snow-white skin, dark hair, bright eyes), thriving in icy neons, jewel tones, and stark black-and-white. 2. Spring: Warm undertones, light-to-medium value, clear chroma, thriving in peachy corals, buttery yellows, and grass greens. 3. Summer: Cool undertones, low contrast, muted chroma, thriving in soft pastels, dusty mauves, and slate blues. 4. Autumn: Warm undertones, low-to-medium contrast, rich earthy chroma, thriving in rust, mustard, olive green, and terracotta. The framework was elegant, intuitive, and became an international sensation. However, it suffered from a massive, systemic blind spot that rendered it virtually useless for the vast majority of the world’s population."
