        "body": "Almost everyone who has attended an Indian wedding, festive party, or family reception has experienced the camera flash shock: you spent an hour grooming in front of the mirror, your outfit looked harmonious, and your skin looked radiant under warm room lighting. Yet when the official wedding photographs arrive, or when someone snaps an impromptu group photo with a phone flash, your face appears startlingly ghost-like, oily, or unrecognizably washed out. This common frustration leads to an important technical question: Does camera flash genuinely change how your skin tone appears, or is your camera simply recording what was always there? From the standpoint of optical physics, camera flash dramatically alters your perceived skin tone. In fact, a direct burst of artificial flash changes virtually every physical parameter of light hitting your face: its intensity, its Correlated Color Temperature (CCT), its directional angle, and the way ambient skin pigments reflect photons back to the digital sensor. To master how you look in festive and evening photography, you need to understand the physics of artificial flash, why South Asian skin is uniquely susceptible to flash distortion, and how to choose colors and fabrics that maintain their dignity under high-intensity strobe lighting."
